For every elliptic curve $E/\mathbb Q$ and each $j\in\{0,1\}$, $$\lim_{X\to\infty}
 \frac{\#\{d\in\mathcal D(X):a(E^{(d)})=j\}}{\#\mathcal D(X)}
 =\frac12.$$

hypotheses.statement:
For every elliptic curve $E/\mathbb Q$ and each $j\in\{0,1\}$, $$\lim_{X\to\infty}
 \frac{\#\{d\in\mathcal D(X):a(E^{(d)})=j\}}{\#\mathcal D(X)}
 =\frac12.$$ Consequently the twists of analytic rank at least two have density zero. For a density-one set of signed squarefree parameters $d$, the analytic and Mordell–Weil ranks of $E^{(d)}$ agree and its whole Tate–Shafarevich group is finite.

Suppose that for arbitrarily large $b$ there is a family $$\label{eq:cy:cube}
 h_x=h_0\prod_{q\in\mathcal Q}(q^*)^{\lambda_q(x)},
 \qquad x\in\mathbb F_2^b,\qquad \lambda_q\in(\mathbb F_2^b)^*,$$ where $\mathcal Q$ consists of distinct primes outside $2Nh_0$, $q^*=(-1/q)q$, and every $h_x$ has the same sign and the same local squareclasses at $2N$.

outputs.exact_statement:
For every elliptic curve $E/\mathbb Q$ and each $j\in\{0,1\}$, $$\lim_{X\to\infty}
 \frac{\#\{d\in\mathcal D(X):a(E^{(d)})=j\}}{\#\mathcal D(X)}
 =\frac12.$$ Consequently the twists of analytic rank at least two have density zero. For a density-one set of signed squarefree parameters $d$, the analytic and Mordell–Weil ranks of $E^{(d)}$ agree and its whole Tate–Shafarevich group is finite.

There is a constant $C_E$, depending only on $E$, with the following property. Fix an odd fundamental discriminant $h_0$, prime to $2N$, with $c_2(E^{(h_0)})=0$. Suppose that for arbitrarily large $b$ there is a family $$\label{eq:cy:cube}
 h_x=h_0\prod_{q\in\mathcal Q}(q^*)^{\lambda_q(x)},
 \qquad x\in\mathbb F_2^b,\qquad \lambda_q\in(\mathbb F_2^b)^*,$$ where $\mathcal Q$ consists of distinct primes outside $2Nh_0$, $q^*=(-1/q)q$, and every $h_x$ has the same sign and the same local squareclasses at $2N$. Assume that a constant $B$, independent of $b$, satisfies $$\label{eq:cy:punctured-bound}
 L(E^{(h_x)},1)\ne0,\qquad D(h_x)\le B\qquad(x\ne0).$$ If $E[2]$ is irreducible, assume also that some $L$, independent of $b$, satisfies $$\label{eq:cy:prime-bound}
 v_2(q^2-1)\le L\qquad(q\in\mathcal Q).$$ Then $L(E^{(h_0)},1)\ne0$ and $$\label{eq:cy:transferred-bound}
 D(h_0)\le B+C_E.$$ The necessary dimension may depend on $h_0$, its finite Selmer group, and the bound in Equation <a href="#eq:cy:prime-bound" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:cy:prime-bound">[eq:cy:prime-bound]</a>; $C_E$ does not.

For either set of data, $$\label{eq:h:height}
 \widehat h(P_c(e))
 =C_E\,c\sqrt{|k|}\,
       \left[L(E^{(e)},s)L(E^{(ek)},s)\right]'_{s=1},$$ with a fixed nonzero constant determined by the parametrization and height conventions. In particular, a simple zero of the product gives total Mordell–Weil rank one and finite Shafarevich–Tate groups.*



*Suppose $h$ has analytic rank one, and use genus data with a fixed partner $h_*$ of analytic rank zero and ring data with a possibly varying $k$, where $L(E^{(hk)},1)\ne0$. Then $$\label{eq:h:index-comparison}
 2j(P_{|h|}(h))-2j(P_1(h_*))
       =v(\mathcal L(hk))+O_{E,h_*}(1).$$ Here the two points are formed in their respective quadratic fields. If instead $h_*$ has analytic rank one and $L(E^{(h)},1)\ne0$, genus data give $$\label{eq:h:reverse-comparison}
 2j(P_1(h_*))=v(\mathcal L(h))+O_{E,h_*}(1).$$

In ring data, suppose the product in Equation <a href="#eq:h:height" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:h:height">[eq:h:height]</a> has a simple zero, every $q\mid h$ splits in $K$, and $\omega(k)\le a$, where $\omega$ counts distinct prime factors. Then $$\label{eq:h:ring-bound}
 2j(P_{|h|}(h))\ge
       4w(h)+s(h)+s(hk)-O_{E,a}(1).$$ There is no irreducibility assumption on $E[2]$.

A network satisfies $f(z,\mathbf1)=\mathcal N P(z)$, where $\mathcal N$ is a sum of terminal finite-difference operators indexed by forests. In each surviving forest term, every component containing an internal primary vertex meets a terminal. A component meeting $t$ terminals has contraction degree $t-1$; a component containing two simple terminals acts as zero. Networks with disjoint internal vertices and no edges between them multiply as operators on the same $P$. In particular, degree greater than $\deg P$ annihilates it.*


*For the grounded Laplacian $Q$ and terminal columns $c_a$ defined above, the constant and the pair-contraction coefficients are $$\label{gr:forest-coefficients}
 \det Q,\qquad c_a^{\mathsf t}\operatorname{adj}(Q)c_b\quad(a\ne b).$$ The latter multiplies the terminal difference in the selected alignment; a prohibited pair multiplies the zero operator.

Fix the curve, support types, a bound for the number of additional constrained places, and a bound for the dimension of a stationary environment. Partition a finite configuration into blocks with zero cross-$M$ entries, and denote their matrices by $M_i$. There is a constant depending only on these fixed data such that $$\label{eq:lm:selmer-nullity}
 \dim_{\mathbb F_2}\mathop{\mathrm{Sel}}_2(E^{(h)}/\mathbb Q)
 \geq\sum_i\dim_{\mathbb F_2}\ker M_i-C.$$ At a unit coefficient symbol the sum of nullities, and hence the number of singular blocks, is therefore uniformly bounded. The bound works for both coefficient sources and is independent of the moving prime $\ell$.

Let $f$ be the weight-two newform of an elliptic curve of conductor $N$, with its $L$-function normalized to have center $1/2$. Fix $H$ divisible by $8$ and $N$, a reduced class $a\pmod H$, and $\sigma\in\{1,-1\}$ with $\sigma a\equiv1\pmod4$. Suppose the twists with $n>0$ squarefree and $n\equiv a\pmod H$ have root number $-1$. For every nonnegative, nonzero $\Phi\in C_c^\infty((1,2))$, there are $c>0$, $c_1\in\mathbb R$, and an absolute $\eta>0$ such that $$\label{odd:moment}
 \sum_{\substack{n>0\ \mathrm{squarefree}\\n\equiv a\ (H)}}
 L'(1/2,f\otimes\chi_{\sigma n})\Phi(n/X)
 =cX\log X+c_1X+O_{f,H,a,\sigma,\Phi}(X^{1-\eta}).$$ Consequently this progression contains arbitrarily large $n$ whose twists have a simple central zero.

Let $f,d\in O[G]$ and let $u_x\in\Lambda[1/2]$ satisfy $d_x=f_xu_x$ at each character. Suppose $f_0\in\Lambda$ and $c_0=v(f_0(0))<\infty$. Fix $M>c_0$, and suppose every $f_x\bmod2^M$ has no powers below $-L(M)$. If $$\label{eq:h:cube-threshold}
 b>(L(M)+2)(2^{M-1}-1),$$ some $x\ne0$ satisfies $$\label{eq:h:central-transfer}
 v\bigl(u_x(0)-u_0(0)\bigr)\ge M-2c_0.$$ Consequently, when $c_0$ and each $L(M)$ have bounds independent of the family, a uniform upper bound $v(u_x(0))\le B_1$ at all nonzero vertices forces $v(u_0(0))\le B_1$ for all sufficiently large $b$. The base value itself is allowed to vary with the family.

Let $\mathcal P$ be a property of nonzero signed integers unchanged by multiplication by an integer square. Suppose $$A(X)=\#\{n\in\mathbb Z:0<|n|\le X,\ \mathcal P(n)\}
      =2\delta X+o(X).$$ Then $$S(X)=\#\{d\in\mathcal D(X):\mathcal P(d)\}
      =\frac{2\delta}{\zeta(2)}X+o(X).$$ In particular the property has density $\delta$ among signed squarefree parameters ordered by absolute value.
